name: the audit
after: 17-permissions
intro: You have been handed a machine and asked what is on it. Answer with
intro: pipelines, not guesses. This is where xos's own guarantees become
intro: something you can check for yourself instead of taking on faith.
---
setup: /bin is full of symlinks to the one busybox binary.
q: count how many names in /bin point at busybox
a: ls -l /bin | grep -c busybox
ref: grep
---
setup: A setuid binary anywhere on a system you did not build is worth a hard
setup: look -- it runs as its owner, not as you. xos fails its own build if one
setup: exists, so on this machine the correct answer is nothing at all.
q: find every setuid file on the whole filesystem
a: find / -perm -4000
a: find / -perm -4000 -type f
ref: find
---
setup: The busiest ref entries are the ones worth reading first.
q: print the ten ref files with the most lines, largest first
a: wc -l /usr/share/learn/ref/* | sort -rn | head -n 10
a: wc -l /usr/share/learn/ref/* | sort -nr | head -n 10
ref: wc
